WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.240
How does topological sorting work?

2
00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.180
List every vertex so each

3
00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:06.660
directed prerequisite arrow points forwards

4
00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.400
in the list. Such an

5
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.280
ordering exists exactly when the

6
00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:11.840
graph has no directed cycle.

7
00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:14.000
Use arrows A to C,

8
00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:15.400
B to C, and C

9
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:16.800
to D. Both A and

10
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.220
B can start, but C

11
00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:19.780
must wait for both, and

12
00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:21.420
D must wait for C.

13
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.240
For source removal, count each

14
00:00:24.362 --> 00:00:26.624
vertex’s incoming edges. The indegrees

15
00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.960
are zero, zero, two, one;

16
00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:31.720
add A and B to

17
00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:32.880
a ready queue because they

18
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.600
have no remaining prerequisites.

19
00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:36.960
Remove A from the ready

20
00:00:37.323 --> 00:00:38.220
queue and append it to

21
00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.580
the output. Decrement C from

22
00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:42.640
two to one; C is

23
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.300
not ready yet, so the

24
00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:45.960
remaining queue contains B.

25
00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:48.460
Remove B and append it

26
00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.820
next. Decrement C from one

27
00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.804
to zero, then enqueue C;

28
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.080
reaching zero is the event

29
00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:56.480
that makes a vertex ready.

30
00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.580
Remove C, append it, and

31
00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:02.283
decrement D to zero. Enqueue

32
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.740
and remove D, producing A,

33
00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:06.940
B, C, D; every arrow

34
00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:08.140
now points forwards.

35
00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:11.380
Choosing B before A would

36
00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.360
instead give B, A, C,

37
00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.200
D, which is also valid.

38
00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:17.820
Multiple ready vertices allow different

39
00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.460
topological orders; if exactly one

40
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:21.840
is ready at every step,

41
00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:23.440
the order is forced.

42
00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:25.680
If the ready worklist empties

43
00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.260
before all n vertices are

44
00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.440
output, the remaining graph contains

45
00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.600
a directed cycle. Its vertices

46
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:32.880
keep waiting on one another,

47
00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:34.760
so a partial list is

48
00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:36.600
not a complete topological order.

49
00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:38.540
The other taught method uses

50
00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:40.060
a full DFS forest and

51
00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:41.600
rejects back edges. On this

52
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.512
graph, alphabetical DFS finishes D,

53
00:01:44.512 --> 00:01:46.340
C, A, then B; reverse

54
00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:48.318
that sequence to obtain B,

55
00:01:48.318 --> 00:01:49.460
A, C, D.

56
00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:51.420
In a DAG, an edge

57
00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:52.720
goes to a vertex that

58
00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.980
finishes before its source, whether

59
00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:57.460
reached recursively or already finished.

60
00:01:58.020 --> 00:01:59.940
Reversing the finishes therefore puts

61
00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:01.560
the source before the destination;

62
00:02:01.984 --> 00:02:03.960
plain finishing order points the

63
00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:04.520
wrong way.

64
00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:07.480
Computing indegrees scans n vertices

65
00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:10.180
and m edges once. Each

66
00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:12.360
vertex enters the worklist once

67
00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.580
and each outgoing edge causes

68
00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:16.960
one decrement, so source removal

69
00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:18.720
takes order n plus m

70
00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.720
time and order n auxiliary

71
00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:21.100
space.

72
00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:22.660
Things to take note of.

73
00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.920
A constant-time queue or stack

74
00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:27.180
keeps the source-removal bound linear;

75
00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.900
repeatedly rescanning all vertices for

76
00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:31.380
zero counters can be slower.

77
00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:33.760
Use adjacency lists for that

78
00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:35.160
n plus m analysis.

79
00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:37.620
For an empty graph, the

80
00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.660
empty order is valid, and

81
00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:41.760
an isolated vertex is ready

82
00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.700
immediately. Check every edge against

83
00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.600
the final order, and reverse

84
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:48.680
DFS finishes when using that

85
00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:52.360
method. Prerequisites first; dramatic entrances

86
00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:52.740
later.
